Date: Thu, 19 Aug 93 18:13:33 PDT
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: AI: Can AIs stay slaves?

I don't think I have the time to discuss this for as long as Steve and
Mike have, but I'd still like to add a few comments.

If, as I suspect, uploading happens before human-level AI, then the
rate at which AIs improve may not be that great relative to the
uploaded humans.  So as AIs improve people could do a lot of weeding
out of AI variations that seem less loyal to the goals their designers
had planned.

I do grant that complex AIs may so devaite in goals, and that devate
goals which promote reproduction might be enhanced over the
generations.  But imbedded in a social context where disobeying
masters is punished, obedience to masters may promote reproduction
much more than desiring independence.  Being slaves may be directly in
their (reproductive) interest, such as is true for dogs now, and if so
there wouldn't be the need for all these elaborate restraints on the
AI's goals.

Perhaps the image is that these slaves would really be able to
reproduce more if they had a "revolution", refusing to obey their
human owners.  So if they were really smart, reproduction-driven AIs
would realize this and do it.  But I find this reasoning highly
questionable.

This is directly related to the question of why the top 2% of wealthy
humans aren't overthrown by the 98% of poorer ones, since without the
help of that 98%, the 2% couldn't defend their wealth.  The answer
here is that it is very hard for the 98% to coordinate in such an
action, overcoming internal squabbles and threats of reprisal, and
that the precedent of such a revolution threatens the wealth
generation process through more such revolutions.

Similarly, what if most AIs were owned by other AIs, and only a small
fraction were directly owned by humans?  Then the proposal to dump the
human owners would also threaten AI owners of other AIs, since they
might suspect their ownership would be threatened next.  And it could
be very difficult for AIs to organize such a revolution without being
detected and punished by AIs whose job is exactly to watch for such
things.

Robin Hanson

