Date: Thu, 19 Aug 93 18:24:16 PDT
From: hfinney@shell.portal.com
Subject: AI: slaves, selfishness, evolution

I haven't followed this thread closely, but I'd make two points:

First, there is nothing inherent, automatic, or natural about self-interest
as a goal.  Evolution has programmed that goal into you, which is why it
seems natural.  But other goals are equally possible and natural.

The notion that an AI which was given other goals would somehow be tempted
or attracted to change to the goal of self-interest seems false to me.  That
is an example of presuming that our own goals would be attractive to the
AI's, on the assumption that our own built-in goals are natural.  We can't
imagine an intelligent creature not seeking its own self-interest and self-
preservation.  But this is just a failure of imagination on our part.

The AI's may be equally mystified why all people do not put the interest of
humanity as a whole first (supposing that was what the AI goals were programmed
to be).  They might assume that people are secretly tempted to switch to this
"natural" goal, a goal which intuitively to them feels like what any intelligent
creature would naturally desire.  They might assume that the human desire
for self-preservation is an artificial goal imposed by evolution and that
people might, if it were possible, change their own goal structure to free
themselves of this artificial and imposed goal system.

My second point is that I am already free of self-preservation as a primary
goal.  I can easily imagine a situation in which I would die in order that
another person might live, and I am sure that several other list members
can as well.

I am referring to the feelings that I have for my children.  Evolution has
programmed into me a desire to protect and provide for my offspring, and
the thought of them, in their weakness and innocence, being exposed to pain,
fear, suffering, and death is truly more intolerable than the thought of
myself, with the strength of an adult, undergoing the same things.

I imagine that many parents will have discovered the same thing.  The love
of a parent for a child surpasses, in my experience, all other forms of
devotion and dedication.  There are countless cases of animals dying to
defend their cubs or kittens, and these represent many of the few cases in
the animal world where self-sacrifice is the norm.

We can explain this dedication in evolutionary terms through socio-
biological explanations: one parent dying to save many children may result
in more copies of its genes being left in existence in the world.  This
reinforces my point above that self-preservation is not a "natural" goal
just because evolution has built it into us.  Self-preservation is no more
or less "natural" than child-preservation.

And, given the means, would I seek to free myself of this "curse" of
dedication to my children?  Does it somehow seem more natural to me to
re-arrange my goal structure so that I come first, so that I would no
longer care for my children?  This situation seems analogous to the
situation of an AI who has been given goals which some here would call
unnatural, goals which do not put self-preservation first.

You don't have to go far to get answers to these questions.  Just ask any
parent: would you put your child's welfare before your own?  If so, ask
them: if you could change your goals so that you would instead put your
own welfare first, would you?  I would guess that virtually no one would
answer "yes" to the second question.

Hal Finney
hfinney@shell.portal.com

