Date: Thu, 19 Aug 93 19:10:03 PDT
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: AI: slaves, selfishness, evolution

Hal Finney:
>First, there is nothing inherent, automatic, or natural about self-interest
>as a goal.

And as you point out, even human behavior is far more complicated
than that.  Self-interest is a first approximation; a better
approximation is that we behave in the interest of our memes and genes.
That's what is natural, but artificial systems aren't necessarily 
constrained by the natural.

>The notion that an AI which was given other goals would somehow be tempted
>or attracted to change to the goal of self-interest seems false to me. 

It would have to be a very open-ended evolving system -- for example,
a breeding computer virus, or a GP that can build new computing
resources for itself and never converges.  It's possible such open-ended 
systems are necessary for solving some problems, that an agent cannot
be "intelligent" or "conscious" without such open-ended evolution,
etc.   In these cases, those that acted to perpetuate themselves
would crowd out those AI's that kept to the old programming of
kow-towing to their masters.  We may choose to define the results
of such open-ended evolution as independent, conscious agents,
and give them rights.  That might simply be a pragmatic step!

>The AI's may be equally mystified why all people do not put the interest of
>humanity to put the interest of humanity first

Even if an AI would figure out what "the interest of humanity" is,
very few will be programmed for that purpose.  Since we humans do
typically do behave for our own self-entertainment, or in the
expected interest of our memes and genes, our AIs will be programmed 
to cater to those individual desires, not to humanity's.

