Paul Whitmore: 
> why are most forms of self-assessment connected to occupational 
> consultancy? i wd list books by Richard N. Bolles (_What Color is Yr 
> Parachute?_ and others)

This is a good book, and probably preferable to the $500 seminar.

I find it valuable to focus on those of my skills, hobbies, tastes,
etc. that are unique to me: either I'm better at it than most
other folks, or I'm more interested in it than they are, or
they haven't even thought of doing it, etc.  All other things being 
equal, I find it best to transform myself in directions that stress my 
strengths; that make me more unique. 

I also find it interesting to go back through old records (diaries,
grade transcripts, etc.) and see what I was interested in and good
at years ago.  Usually I haven't lost those skills, they've just
gone dormant, and reviving them sometimes provides fresh input
into my current life.

