Blech.  I think I just witnessed a space orgasm.   Now for the
cold shower:

> 2004: Extropy Institute takes delivery of first privately-owned Delta
> Clipper heavy-lift SSTO vehicle.

2004:  Feds refuse launch license (munitions control), and
prepare missile defenses to shoot all unauthorized launches on site
(could be terrorists, can't wait to find out).  Since MacDac is
a heavy government contractor, they won't deliver the goods
to a bunch of anarchists anyway.

> 2007: ExI orbital power platform goes online.

2008: Presumably orbital power for space-based operations like direct 
broadcast and phone cell satellites, beam weapons, microgrv/vac/plasma
manufacturing, and the like.  Even if DC achieves $100/kg to LEO (probably 
not), SPS will still be over an order of magnitude too expensive.
However, a wide variety of other orbital industries will benefit
greatly or be made possible by lower launch costs in the near term.  For 
example, if launch costs drop by only a factor of two, phone cell sats 
and DBS (each potentially over $10 billion/year markets) will no longer be 
marginal.   Commercial remote sensing will no longer be marginal.  
Another factor of two, and Clarke-orbit based phone exchanges (installed 
instead of local Baby Bells in currently underdeveloped areas) will no 
longer be marginal.  Etc.

Keep in mind that launch _reliability_ is also very
important.  We want to create a transport industry instead
of a nifty side-use for ICBMs.  A big barrier to space industry
is the current 10%+ rate of failures to get the cargo delivered
to Clarke orbit (geosynchronous), and the accompanying high insurance 
rates.  That's orders of magnitude higher than the failure rate
in other major transport industries (ocean shipping, rail, airlines,
trucking, etc.)

> 2010: ExI buys two more Clippers, begins lifting bootstrap components
> to L-2[1]. Secret mining station on Lunar Farside revealed as Lunar
> materials launched to L-2 rendezvous.

2011: Lacking any sort of water or organic material, the industrial
equipment grinds to a halt and our intrepid explorers die of thirst.
Dead pioneers are trivial to spot with the millions of Brilliant
Eyes spysats launched to every major moon and planet in the solar
system by DC and upper stages.

Meanwhile, a second Extropian expdition has more sensibly ignored 
the pork-barrel attempts to rehash Apollo and has set up shop in the 
Jupiter-family comets: farther out of reach of Earth, and with water, 
organics, propellant, coolant, lubrication, plastics, biotech and nanotech 
raw materials, etc. readily available.  Asteroids full of meteoritic steel 
only a small ice-rocket hop away.

> 2012: ExI redeems first Space Emigration Vouchers as 256 pioneers fly
> 2 to L-2 station to occupy gravitational high ground of Earth-Moon system.

2013: L-2 station taken out by U.N Peacekeepers.  (Iraqis could
have used modified Scuds to take out SSF, except that SSF is still
in the design stage in 2013...)

> [1] If I've mixed up the LaGrange numbers, I'm heartily sorry. 

Gosh I hope not.  Mixing up L-2 and L-5 would violate a sacred
religious rite!

> [2] All space businesses mentioned are either sole proprietorships,
> unlimited partnerships, or joint stock companies of unlimited
> liability.

Except for DC-X itself, (and likely its successors) which
are being (or are planned to be) developed with government funds 
paid to limited-liability corporations.

#END cold_shower_mode

One of these days we'll see a new, Extropian vision of space
colonization.  I hope my upcoming article for Extropy can start
that off.  Unlike the pork-barrel visions of NASA and ideas inspired 
by Apollo in the 1970s, I account for where the most important and
highest-quality raw materials are, where the most lucrative
business opportunities are etc.  Meanwhile, cf. my previous post on 
comet mining for an overview of one of the big new possibilities NASA 
is ignoring.

