I'd wager that in the next 10 years, private space access will be a reality
in the United States. First, its entirely possible that, beyond DC-X, the
SSTO (is that the right acronym?) program will be privately funded. I can
easily see, for the first time, _two_ or more companies developing the same
technology for space exploration in the US. Normally, a technology is
identified, and the US government hands out contracts for each phase from
start to end. Only occasionally are there cases where two companies will
actually spend a great deal of money to compete for the next phase of the
project, in extreme cases resulting in 2 full prototypes.

But anyway, I think that this technology may be the first one (space related)
in this country where you may see the industry actually competing to bring
it to production. Even if the government does stay involved, I think you
may still see competition. Because this technology is so _relatively_ cheap
to develop, its possible to drum up investors and bank loans to cover it,
if the interest is high enough. Given that its likely our only chance at
competing in lift capacity with the rest of the world again, that interest
should exist.

As for government regulations, even if they do keep them up, why not just
launch in Mexico or somewhere? I would expect Extropians to be that last ones
to feel constricted by geographical location. After all, a great deal of
life extension research goes on in other countries, as well as other areas
of interest to Extropians. If the US isn't accomodating, just take your
business elsewhere. If the effort is serious enough, I think the government
is now at the point where it realizes that it needs to head off situations
where the law encourages private industry to go elsewhere to do business.
Start talking about jobs and local economies, and the government will listen,
no matter what you are trying to do. California would be a good target for
such an effort. For the past ?? many years, they've turned away business
after business because of stupid tax laws and labor laws and such. But now,
they are desparate, and know that they have to be _very_ accomodating to
_any_ company which considers creating jobs in California, or at least
conducting business here (which might be taxed).

I hope your article (whatever it is) takes a serious look at what it will
take to get back into space, in a big way. I think it can be done, and soon.
I also think that we need to forget the problems of the past, and to realize
that the time is coming when the door might actually be open for private
industry to get into space.

